when you flip on a tv or stream a game  online to see women competing in sports  across the u.s  understand that's in part the legacy of  title ix  this month marks the 50th anniversary of  the barrier-breaking federal law that  changed the game for gender equity in  education and athletics  first off you may be wondering what  exactly title ix is  this federal civil rights law was  authored chiefly by representative patsy  mink the first woman of color elected to  the u.s congress with support from  representative edith greene and senator  birch bai  the 1972 civil rights legislation states  in part  no person in the united states shall on  the basis of sex be excluded from  participation in be denied benefits of  or be subjected to discrimination under  any education program or activity  receiving federal financial assistance  this law initially focused on ensuring  that women would have equitable  opportunities to receive educations and  earn degrees and it prohibited  discrimination on the basis of gender  identity and sexual orientation as well  as pregnancy parenting and abortion  status the legislation also prohibited  discrimination on the basis of blindness  and visual impairment and provided  protections against sexual harassment  and violence now without the word  activity as a part of the law the impact  of title ix may never have been felt in  sports but the legislation ultimately  pertains to equity in everything from  admissions financial aid and  scholarships to schools having to  provide equally for men's and women's  athletics at the time few women were  admitted into universities and receiving  an athletic scholarship felt like a pipe  dream  to those who did want to compete they  usually had to join intramural club  teams along with shouldering the  financial burden themselves in fact in  1975 mink had to fight to keep athletics  regulations included in title ix with  congress ultimately preserving the  sports component of the legislation  before its passage one in 27 girls  played sports in the united states by  2016 according to the women's sports  foundation  one in every five girls in this country  were participating before title ix  around 32 000 college women participated  in sports as of the 2019-2020 academic  year  over 224 000 women played college sports  per usa today but gender equity still  has a long way to go the existence of  title ix wasn't a magic wand it served  as a new standard violations of which  there would now be greater pressure to  address just last year seven women  including three former athletes brought  a lawsuit against louisiana state  university saying the school mishandled  and in some cases even discouraged title  ix complaints from these women alleging  sex-based discrimination  including rape sexual assault sexual  harassment and or stalking perpetrated  by male lsu students  in early 2022 five female coaches  reached a settlement with colby college  after filing a discrimination complaint  alleging the school provided more  resources to the men's teams and paid  its men's and women's coaches unequally  in 2021 clemson university athletes won  a title ix settlement after both male  and female athletes issued claims that  their title ix protections were being  violated with the men's varsity track  and field and cross-country teams cut  and the women's rowing cross country and  track and field teams reporting that  they weren't receiving equal athletic  financial aid treatments or benefits  the men's and women's teams mobilized  stood together and made a significant  leap toward equality for all  title ix disrupted a system upholding  sexism and transformed girls and women's  athletics forever but we still need to  use the groundbreaking legislation of 50  years ago as inspiration to further  strengthen protections for trans  athletes and sexual assault survivors to  prevent discrimination and hold  institutions accountable because we are  all created equally the law  and the gain  should reflect that
 

